{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## HOW TO SETTLE AC1 — and why the obvious route does NOT work yet\n\nAC1 asks whether DC 8.17.1 returns success or an error for a sub-task `PUT fields.parent` that does\nnot take effect. The obvious route — read the `bridge_alerts` records that change 1305 now writes on\nevery `set_parent` failure arm — **does not work in the harness today**, and I confirmed that rather\nthan assuming it:\n\n    grep -rn \"bridge_alert\" tests/external/live_jira_dc/                    -> nothing\n    grep -rn \"bridge_alerts\" .github/workflows/external-integration.yml     -> nothing\n\n`record_parent_divergence` writes to `<repo_root>/bridge_state/bridge_alerts/<date>.jsonl` in the\nephemeral runner's working repo. Nothing reads it and nothing uploads it, so the file dies with the\nrunner. **An absence of alerts in a harness run is therefore NOT evidence that DC returned success** —\nit only means nobody looked. Recording this explicitly because the inverse inference is tempting and\nwould be the same shape of mistake this epic has already paid for twice.\n\n### Three ways to get the observation, cheapest first\n\n 1. **Read the alert store inside the failing cell.** The cell already has the working repo path\n    (`dc_store_copy_repo`); after the outbound pass it can glob\n    `bridge_state/bridge_alerts/*.jsonl` and assert on the records. Smallest change, no workflow\n    edit, and it makes the distinction an ASSERTION rather than a log a human has to read.\n 2. **Probe the platform directly**, bypassing rebar entirely: `PUT /rest/api/2/issue/{subtask}`\n    with a new parent over raw REST, capture the HTTP status and body, then re-read\n    `fields.parent`. This answers the platform question in isolation and does not depend on any\n    rebar code path being correct — worth doing FIRST, because if DC returns 400 the whole\n    accept-and-ignore reading collapses and option 1 becomes unnecessary.\n 3. Dump/upload the alert directory as a workflow artifact. Most general, but it makes the evidence\n    something a human must go and read instead of something the suite asserts, so it is the weakest\n    of the three despite being the most reusable.\n\nOption 2 then option 1 is the order I would take: establish the platform fact with a direct probe,\nthen encode whichever answer it gives as an assertion in the cell so it cannot regress silently.\n\n### A follow-on worth noting\n\nWhichever option is taken, the underlying gap is general: rebar's only operator-visible signal for a\nswallowed mutation failure is invisible to the very suite built to catch swallowed mutation failures.\nEvery bug in this family (d067, 8d68, 751e, 2b16, 88d9, 39c1, and this one) was a silent success. A\nharness that cannot see the silence-breaking channel will keep needing per-bug forensics."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785781577867599001,"uuid":"8cee1833-5975-48e6-a188-eb21a7f1c5e1"}